package com.example.sap_cpi.Differences

In SAP Cloud Integration (CPI), both FTP and SFTP are file transfer protocols, but SFTP offers
significantly enhanced security. FTP transfers data in plain text, while SFTP encrypts data during
transmission, protecting against unauthorized access and complying with stricter security regulations.

Key Differences:

Security:
SFTP utilizes SSH (Secure Shell) for secure file transfer, encrypting data and authentication details.
FTP, on the other hand, does not offer encryption, leaving data vulnerable.

Port Usage:
SFTP typically uses a single port (usually 22) for all communication, simplifying firewall configuration.
FTP can use multiple ports for control and data connections, potentially creating firewall issues.

Authentication:
SFTP supports both password-based and public key authentication, providing stronger security options.
FTP relies primarily on username and password authentication.

Encryption:
SFTP provides full end-to-end encryption, ensuring data confidentiality during transmission.
FTP does not offer any encryption.

Firewall Friendliness:
SFTP's use of a single port makes it easier to configure in environments with firewalls.

Resumable Transfers:
SFTP supports resumable transfers, meaning you can resume a transfer if interrupted, while FTP does not.

In SAP CPI:
You can use the FTP and SFTP adapters to connect to various FTP and SFTP servers for file transfer tasks.
The SFTP adapter in CPI uses SSH protocol to transfer files securely, encrypting sensitive information before transmission.
For secure file transfers, SFTP is the preferred protocol in CPI, as it provides better security and compliance with
security standards.